23:03:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I see the confusion! You’re correct that once the plane reaches cruising altitude, the pressure inside the cabin is held fairly steady for the majority of the flight. But that steady pressure is *not* the same as the pressure on the ground — it’s lower. And during climb and descent, it’s actively changing. That’s when your ears pop. Think of it this way: - **Takeoff and climb**: The cabin pressure is gradually reduced from ground-level pressure (about 14.7 psi at sea level) to a lower cruising pressure (equivalent to about 6,000–8,000 feet altitude). So the pressure outside your eardrum is *falling* while the pressure inside your middle ear is still at ground level. The eardrum bulges outward, and you feel the need to pop your ears to let the higher internal pressure escape through the Eustachian tube. This is usually a series of pops as the plane climbs. - **Cruise**: Once the plane reaches its cruising altitude, the cabin pressure stabilizes at that lower-but-constant level. Your ears have (hopefully) already equalized, so you typically feel no further popping. The pressure is consistent, yes — but it’s consistently *lower* than at takeoff. That’s why you might notice your ears feel “full” or slightly plugged, but not actively popping. - **Descent and landing**: The cabin pressure is gradually increased back up to the pressure at your destination airport. Now the pressure outside your eardrum is *rising*, pushing the eardrum inward. The air inside your middle ear is at the lower cruise pressure, so you need to let air *in* through the Eustachian tube to equalize. This is often more noticeable (and sometimes more painful) than on the way up because the Eustachian tubes can be slower to open when pressure is pushing inward. So it’s the *changing* pressure during climb and descent that makes your ears pop, even though the pressure is held steady during cruise. The popping is purely due to the pressure difference between your middle ear and