23:03:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very common assumption, and it makes intuitive sense—so you’re not alone in thinking that. However, the cabin pressure is **not** kept at sea-level pressure during the flight. It’s kept at a lower pressure, equivalent to an altitude of about 6,000–8,000 feet. Here’s why: ### 1. Structural limitations If the cabin were pressurized to sea-level pressure (14.7 psi) while the outside air pressure at 35,000 feet is only about 3.8 psi, the pressure difference across the fuselage would be enormous. The aircraft’s aluminum or composite skin would need to be much thicker and heavier to withstand that constant high stress. By keeping the inside pressure lower (say, 10.9 psi, which matches the pressure at 8,000 feet), the pressure difference is reduced, allowing the fuselage to be lighter and more efficient. That’s a huge fuel saving over the life of the plane. ### 2. Safety and comfort Cabin altitude (the apparent altitude inside) is specifically regulated. Aviation authorities (like the FAA) require that the cabin altitude does not exceed 8,000 feet under normal operations. Most cruise cabin altitudes are between 6,000 and 8,000 feet. This is a compromise between passenger comfort (most healthy people tolerate this pressure without any issues, though some may feel mild discomfort) and structural weight. Pressurizing to sea level would be possible in principle, but it’s simply too costly in weight and fuel—so it isn’t done commercially. ### 3. Your ear-popping proof If the cabin stayed at sea-level pressure throughout the flight, you wouldn’t feel any ear pops *at all* during descent, because the pressure inside would exactly match the ground pressure you’re used to. But you do feel them—often more on landing than takeoff. That’s because as the plane descends, the cabin pressure is being raised from that cruising altitude (6,000–8,000 ft) back up to the destination’s ground pressure (which might be near sea level, or higher if you’re landing at a high-elevation airport). So the ear-popping is direct evidence that the pressure changes. So, to directly address your thought: - **Cabin pressure is consistent *during cruise***, yes—but not at ground pressure; it’s lower. - **It changes between gate and cruise**—decreasing on climb, then increasing on descent. - That’s exactly what your ears are reacting to. You’re absolutely right that the ear-popping needs a pressure change—and that change exists precisely because the cabin isn’t maintained at sea-level pressure. I hope that clarifies why airplanes don’t keep it at ground level!
